/* Watch tab data. Summaries are paraphrased; videos play from YouTube (not re-hosted). Built by kb/build_watch.py */
window.WATCH={"hughes":[{"id":"GPWZ4aAtt3c","title":"Elizabeth Holmes’ interviews","who":"Elizabeth Holmes, Theranos founder (later convicted of fraud), in TV and conference interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Nov 2021","dur":"1:50:20","sum":"The panel works through several Holmes interviews. They point to a voice that seems put on, near-constant raised brows, and her switching between ‘I’ and ‘we’ when credit or blame comes up. They treat these as signs of pressure and image management, not proof by themselves.","concepts":["Deception cues","Needs & approval","Pronouns & words","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"5:50","label":"‘I’ vs ‘we’","note":"Pronouns shift with whether she is taking credit or sharing responsibility.","cue":"","ask":"pronoun-world"},{"t":"36:12","label":"Lips tighten under a money question","note":"Lip tightening and a chin movement appear right as company value is questioned.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"1:00:48","label":"Mouth snaps shut after statements","note":"Hughes flags an immediate mouth closure as soon as an answer ends.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"1:14:58","label":"Raised brows, constant nodding","note":"Brows stay up and she nods throughout: read as wanting approval.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"1:30:10","label":"Request for approval","note":"She checks how she is being received, sits more rigidly and stops using her hands.","cue":"","ask":"need-approval"}]},{"id":"LJfRU7FCmW4","title":"Chris Watts’ TV interviews","who":"Chris Watts, who later pleaded guilty to murdering his family (Colorado, 2018), in the interviews he gave before his arrest","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Feb 2021","dur":"1:32:02","sum":"The panel reads Watts’ porch interviews from before his arrest. Hughes scores several answers on his deception scale, counting cues such as a hidden inhale, lip licks, one-shoulder shrugs and the loss of pronouns. They show how many cues pile up in a few seconds, and they compare each one with his own baseline.","concepts":["Deception cues","Clusters","Baseline","Pronouns & words","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"16:51","label":"Cluster before an answer","note":"A hidden inhale, a lip lick, a one-shoulder shrug and a head shake come before he answers. Hughes scores it on his scale.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"drs-scale"},{"t":"17:43","label":"Pronouns disappear","note":"Describing the house, he suddenly stops using pronouns. Hughes reads that as distancing.","cue":"Drops 'I' from the story","ask":"pronoun-world"},{"t":"26:52","label":"Brow raise for approval","note":"As he says he called her several times, his brows lift and he glances to see if he is believed.","cue":"Glance to a third person","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"48:01","label":"Swallow, lip retraction, eye blocking","note":"Several stress cues land within the first three seconds of an answer.","cue":"Throat rises / hard swallow","ask":"cue-throat-rises-hard-swallow"},{"t":"1:28:12","label":"Using his baseline","note":"The panel sums up how the cues changed from his normal behaviour across the clips.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"uGbU84dLVNU","title":"Chris Watts: police body-cam footage","who":"Chris Watts, filmed on police body cameras on the day his family was reported missing (2018)","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Mar 2021","dur":"1:36:50","sum":"The body-cam footage shows Watts talking with officers and neighbours. The panel notes repeated eyebrow flashes, an open mouth and fast breathing, which they read as rising fight-or-flight. Hughes counts the cues on his scale and shows how fast a blink rate can jump when a key question lands.","concepts":["Deception cues","Stress cues","Clusters","Baseline","Needs & approval"],"moments":[{"t":"6:24","label":"Eyebrow flash for approval","note":"He keeps flashing his brows as if asking to be believed.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"17:30","label":"Mouth open for air","note":"An open mouth is read as the body pulling in more oxygen as stress rises.","cue":"Nostril flare","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"22:05","label":"Fast breathing","note":"They estimate a very high breathing rate at this point.","cue":"Belly → chest breathing","ask":"cue-belly-chest-breathing"},{"t":"59:02","label":"Scoring the cluster","note":"Hughes counts rocking, hidden hands, eyebrow flashes, a raised blink rate and an open mouth on his scale.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"drs-scale"},{"t":"1:18:33","label":"Blink-rate jump","note":"His blink rate jumps sharply as soon as the final question is asked.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"}]},{"id":"5t1jOZTe7iU","title":"Jodi Arias interrogation","who":"Jodi Arias, later convicted of murder (Arizona, 2013), in her police interrogation","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Feb 2021","dur":"1:48:18","sum":"The panel looks at the language and the face in Arias’ interrogation. Qualifiers tacked onto her answers, raised brows and lips that close fast after she speaks get the most attention, along with downward glances read as rehearsing what to say next.","concepts":["Deception cues","Pronouns & words","Needs & approval","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"9:12","label":"Qualifier tacked on","note":"An extra phrase added to an answer to make it sound more convincing.","cue":"'As far as I know', 'basically'","ask":"verbal-deception"},{"t":"29:10","label":"Qualifiers everywhere","note":"Almost every answer comes with a qualifier.","cue":"'As far as I know', 'basically'","ask":"verbal-deception"},{"t":"48:17","label":"Chin up, lips back","note":"Her chin lifts and her lips draw back, read as a request for approval.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"1:11:29","label":"Lips close fast","note":"Her lips close quickly right after she finishes speaking.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"1:37:10","label":"Eyes down, buying time","note":"A downward glance is read as her preparing the next line.","cue":"Unusual pause before answering","ask":"deception-is-stress"}]},{"id":"KofNh6YSFF8","title":"Scott Peterson’s TV interviews","who":"Scott Peterson, convicted in 2004 of murdering his wife Laci (his appeals continue), in TV interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Feb 2021","dur":"1:19:07","sum":"The panel breaks down Peterson’s TV interviews. They pick out lip compression, smiles that don’t fit the topic, one-shoulder shrugs and the lack of pronouns. They also warn that a smile can be part of someone’s baseline.","concepts":["Deception cues","Baseline","Pronouns & words","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"12:30","label":"Smile under pressure","note":"They caution that some people smile under stress as part of their normal baseline.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"13:05","label":"Lip compression","note":"His lips squeeze together, read as holding something back.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"14:34","label":"Smile grows on a hard word","note":"When a heavy word comes up, his smile widens. They read that as nervous energy being released.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"21:04","label":"Textbook one-shoulder shrug","note":"A one-shoulder shrug comes during a key statement, read as low confidence in what he is saying.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"1:09:11","label":"No pronouns","note":"A stretch of talk with no pronouns at all, which Hughes treats as a strong verbal flag.","cue":"Drops 'I' from the story","ask":"pronoun-world"}]},{"id":"PFYTBMfVqr0","title":"Prince Andrew’s BBC Newsnight interview","who":"Prince Andrew, in his 2019 BBC interview about his links to Jeffrey Epstein (he denies wrongdoing)","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Jun 2020","dur":"1:14:09","sum":"This is a cue-by-cue read of the 2019 BBC interview. The panel shows one-sided contempt, changes in breathing you can see from the shoulders, and a shrug plus brow flash and head tilt that they read as approval-seeking. Overall they describe the delivery as well rehearsed.","concepts":["Stress cues","Needs & approval","Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"8:18","label":"One-sided (contempt) expression","note":"Contempt is the one expression that shows on only one side of the face.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"10:59","label":"Breathing rises","note":"You can see his breathing speed up from the movement at his shoulder.","cue":"Belly → chest breathing","ask":"cue-belly-chest-breathing"},{"t":"18:47","label":"Shrug, then a brow flash","note":"A shrug as he moves into his narrative, then a brow flash and head tilt asking for approval.","cue":"Head tilt","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"1:09:57","label":"Rehearsed delivery","note":"The panel compares this with his baseline and calls the delivery almost perfectly executed.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"QQRaEm321gs","title":"Belle Gibson’s 60 Minutes interview","who":"Belle Gibson, who falsely claimed to have cured her cancer (an Australian court later fined her), in her 2015 60 Minutes interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Jan 2022","dur":"1:15:24","sum":"The panel looks at Gibson’s 60 Minutes interview. They point to small expressions of disgust, breathing you can follow at her collar and a mouth that closes the moment an answer ends. Hughes scores a few seconds of one answer on his behaviour table before she even speaks.","concepts":["Deception cues","Clusters","Baseline","Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"3:15","label":"A flicker of disgust","note":"A small disgust movement at the mouth.","cue":"Disgust","ask":"cue-disgust"},{"t":"4:18","label":"Breathing at the collar","note":"Watch her polo neck rise and fall to track her breathing rate.","cue":"Belly → chest breathing","ask":"cue-belly-chest-breathing"},{"t":"7:16","label":"Deviation from her baseline","note":"An immediate mouth closure after a statement stood out from how she normally behaves.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"57:15","label":"Vague words and qualifiers","note":"Vague language, qualifiers and hesitation in a short answer.","cue":"'As far as I know', 'basically'","ask":"verbal-deception"},{"t":"1:01:45","label":"Scored before she speaks","note":"Breathing shifts to the chest and her blink rate rises. Hughes scores this on his table before she answers.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"drs-scale"}]},{"id":"Vzoy54gMitc","title":"John Wayne Gacy: FBI interview tapes","who":"John Wayne Gacy, convicted serial killer (executed in 1994), in recorded interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Apr 2022","dur":"1:27:36","sum":"The panel sets up Gacy’s eye-movement baseline and then watches for changes. They note head tilts, lip retraction and chin movement (shame or grief), contempt expressions, distancing language and a switch to ‘we’ that softens his own role.","concepts":["Baseline","Deception cues","Pronouns & words","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"4:34","label":"Setting his baseline","note":"Where his eyes go during normal recall becomes the reference point.","cue":"Looks to one side for good memories","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"15:11","label":"Unusual head tilt","note":"A head tilt that isn’t part of his baseline, at a key statement.","cue":"Head tilt","ask":"cue-head-tilt"},{"t":"15:52","label":"Lip retraction, chin movement","note":"Lip retraction and chin movement, read as shame or grief, alongside distancing words.","cue":"Lips pulled in / bitten","ask":"cue-lips-pulled-in-bitten"},{"t":"40:58","label":"Contempt, then a shrug","note":"A contempt expression, then a one-shoulder shrug as he dismisses it.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"48:53","label":"Switch to ‘we’","note":"Team pronouns soften his own part in the story.","cue":"Drops 'I' from the story","ask":"pronoun-world"}]},{"id":"xkm0vx0xiaI","title":"Richard Kuklinski (‘the Iceman’) interview","who":"Richard Kuklinski, convicted contract killer, in a prison documentary interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Dec 2021","dur":"1:08:34","sum":"The panel warns that a baseline can itself be a deceptive one, so they keep asking questions. They point to small contempt flashes and a rare moment where chin trembling betrays emotion he is trying to swallow.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"3:19","label":"A baseline for deception?","note":"They warn that the baseline you collect might be someone’s deceptive baseline, so you keep asking.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"36:49","label":"Mild contempt","note":"His lip rises slightly at the end of a story.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"51:30","label":"Chin trembles","note":"Chin and lower-mouth trembling as he chokes back emotion.","cue":"","ask":""}]},{"id":"moKoQPAyrWs","title":"Richard Ramirez (‘Night Stalker’) interview","who":"Richard Ramirez, convicted serial killer, in a jailhouse interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Jan 2021","dur":"1:16:11","sum":"Hughes picks out genuine smiles, which come when Ramirez is compared with other notorious killers. The panel reads that, together with his chin rising, as pride and a need for significance. They also cover a fixed, low-blink stare and lines that sound rehearsed but unpractised.","concepts":["Needs & approval","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"14:12","label":"Genuine smile at recognition","note":"He gives a real smile when he is ranked alongside other killers.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"need-significance"},{"t":"18:07","label":"Fixed, low-blink stare","note":"A fixed stare with very little blinking.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"23:04","label":"Chin rises with pride","note":"His chin lifts when he is compared with others: significance.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"need-significance"},{"t":"49:14","label":"Brow raise, then disdain","note":"A brow raise looking for approval, followed by disdain.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"}]},{"id":"XZuvFL_u0yg","title":"Anna Delvey (Sorokin) interviews","who":"Anna Sorokin (‘Anna Delvey’), convicted of fraud in New York in 2019, in interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Mar 2022","dur":"2:04:13","sum":"The panel looks at whether Sorokin’s confidence is real. They build her eye-accessing baseline, then flag pride displays (chin up, head back), brow raises asking for approval and small contempt expressions. They also explain why baseline matters so much.","concepts":["Baseline","Needs & approval","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"14:15","label":"Eye-accessing baseline","note":"Where her eyes go during normal recall becomes the reference point.","cue":"Looks to one side for good memories","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"29:25","label":"Pride display","note":"Her chin goes up and her head goes back with a smile, read as pride.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"need-significance"},{"t":"30:02","label":"Brow raise for approval","note":"A repeating pattern of brow raises as she seeks approval.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"55:41","label":"Expressions that fade","note":"When she is more honest, her mouth stays open and expressions fade slowly instead of vanishing.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"1:14:04","label":"Micro-contempt","note":"A tiny contempt expression, possibly aimed at her old self.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"}]},{"id":"1VAkvSFDsuk","title":"Robinhood CEO under questioning","who":"Vlad Tenev, Robinhood CEO, in a 2021 interview about the GameStop trading halt (not accused of a crime)","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Feb 2021","dur":"1:07:29","sum":"This is a good example of pressure without any crime. The panel reads Tenev’s constant brow raises and repeated shrugs as apologetic and approval-seeking, and points to a big swallow when the interviewer challenges him. They also show how to watch breathing at the throat notch.","concepts":["Stress cues","Needs & approval","Baseline"],"moments":[{"t":"4:08","label":"Shrug, head shake, eye block","note":"Three cues together as he denies a claim.","cue":"Both shoulders shrug","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"4:56","label":"Approval-seeking tone","note":"Constant brow raises and an apologetic tone throughout.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"11:56","label":"Big swallow","note":"A visible swallow when he is told he is doing the opposite of what the business promises.","cue":"Throat rises / hard swallow","ask":"cue-throat-rises-hard-swallow"},{"t":"20:23","label":"Watch the throat notch","note":"How to read breathing rate from the notch at the base of the throat.","cue":"Belly → chest breathing","ask":"cue-belly-chest-breathing"},{"t":"42:23","label":"Shrug plus a confirmation glance","note":"A one-shoulder shrug, then a glance at the host to check how it landed.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"}]},{"id":"fdXv-AdCksc","title":"Biden vs Trump: first 2020 debate","who":"Joe Biden and Donald Trump at the first 2020 presidential debate","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Oct 2020","dur":"1:04:05","sum":"This is a debate breakdown rather than a lie hunt. The panel watches who backs away to make space, mouth movements and blink rate rising under attack, rehearsed lines versus lines delivered under stress, and hands that stop matching the speech rhythm once a speaker is interrupted.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"26:47","label":"Creating distance","note":"Backing away to create space is read as a stress response.","cue":"Leans away","ask":"proxemics"},{"t":"30:53","label":"Smile drops, blinks rise","note":"A broad smile turns into pursed lips, and blinks rise as soon as he is attacked.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"42:35","label":"Flashes of contempt","note":"Brief contempt expressions during exchanges.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"43:59","label":"Hands lose the beat","note":"On message, his hands move in time with his words. The more he is interrupted, the less they match.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"Rcy7n_HOguk","title":"Will Smith and Chris Rock at the Oscars","who":"Will Smith and Chris Rock at the 2022 Oscars","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Apr 2022","dur":"20:08","sum":"This short episode looks at what came before and after the slap. Rock’s hands go behind his back as he takes it, and Smith shifts his balance onto his dominant foot. The panel’s safety tip is to watch the shoulders, because people have to move them before any physical action.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"8:10","label":"Hands behind the back","note":"Rock takes it the way a soldier takes a dressing-down, hands behind his back.","cue":"Hands behind back","ask":"cue-hands-behind-back"},{"t":"12:31","label":"Weight onto the dominant foot","note":"Smith shifts his balance as if ready to fight.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"18:31","label":"Watch shoulders before violence","note":"Anyone about to move has to move their shoulders first.","cue":"Shoulders stay raised","ask":""}]},{"id":"iksaUTFRhT4","title":"What ‘on message’ looks like: Biden at the 2020 debate","who":"Joe Biden at the 2020 presidential debates","src":"Chase Hughes on Newsmax (his YouTube channel)","by":"Chase Hughes, interviewed by Grant Stinchfield","date":"Oct 2020","dur":"3:09","sum":"A three-minute TV clip in which Hughes explains what he looks for. Rehearsed, on-message lines come with deliberate eye contact with the camera and even, symmetrical gestures that keep time with the speech. He stresses that no single behaviour means deception or honesty: you are reading comfort and stress.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"0:07","label":"On-message behaviour","note":"Deliberate eye contact and symmetrical gestures that keep time with the words.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"0:47","label":"No single ‘lie’ behaviour","note":"Hughes says there is no one behaviour for honesty or deception: look for comfort or stress.","cue":"","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"1:45","label":"Turning to the camera","note":"Looking straight into the camera is read as a scripted, rehearsed line.","cue":"","ask":""}]},{"id":"6VLZWC90eXQ","title":"R. Kelly’s interview with Gayle King","who":"R. Kelly, convicted of racketeering and sex trafficking (2021) and child sex crimes (2022), in his 2019 CBS interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Apr 2021","dur":"1:32:02","sum":"The panel reads Kelly’s eyebrow flashes as requests for approval, which they say celebrities use a lot. They track where he places past and future on his timeline. Around his denials they see a large cluster: head shakes, eye blocks, lip suppression, a one-shoulder shrug and turtling. They also remind viewers that clusters, not single cues, carry weight.","concepts":["Clusters","Needs & approval","Deception cues"],"moments":[{"t":"10:15","label":"Brow flash for approval","note":"Celebrities often use brow flashes to win approval.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"},{"t":"32:05","label":"You need a cluster","note":"A reminder that it takes a cluster to call truth or lie.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"46:29","label":"A big cluster","note":"Head shakes, eye blocks, lip suppression, self-soothing, a one-shoulder shrug and turtling, all together.","cue":"Turtling (shoulders up, head down)","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"35:41","label":"Chin-up indignation","note":"He lifts his chin in a show of indignation.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":""}]},{"id":"-M-_u45wgTc","title":"Andrew Cuomo’s apology statement","who":"Andrew Cuomo, then New York governor, in his 2021 video statement after a report on harassment allegations (he denied wrongdoing and resigned)","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Aug 2021","dur":"1:51:44","sum":"The panel uses Cuomo’s statement to show baseline work. A tongue-jut is just his normal preparation to speak, not a tell. Against that, they count his blink rate, note a sideways smirk at the word ‘inappropriately’ and a mouth that snaps shut, and read his shoulders punctuating the lines as reading from a screen.","concepts":["Baseline","Stress cues","Emotion","Needs & approval"],"moments":[{"t":"8:53","label":"Sideways smirk","note":"A one-sided smirk as he says the word ‘inappropriately’.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"},{"t":"21:03","label":"Counting blinks","note":"Hughes counts a high blink rate over less than a minute.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"22:18","label":"Part of his baseline","note":"A tongue movement that is just his normal preparation to speak. Not a tell.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:05:29","label":"Mouth snaps shut","note":"An immediate mouth closure that differs from his other answers.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"1:09:13","label":"Brows up","note":"Brows go up: a request for approval, or emphasis.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"}]},{"id":"ldIVCDFlFms","title":"Erin Patterson’s police interview","who":"Erin Patterson, convicted in 2025 of the mushroom-lunch murders (Victoria, Australia), in her police interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Sep 2025","dur":"29:24","sum":"Hughes uses a cabinet in the background as a reference point to show how her eyes move away right after a relevant question. Then he explains what a cluster is: eye blocking, very restrained nodding and strong lip compression. The panel also notes she answers the question she thinks is being asked, not the one that was put to her.","concepts":["Clusters","Baseline","Deception cues","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"16:19","label":"A visual reference point","note":"He uses a background object to track where her eyes go after each question.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"16:33","label":"What a cluster is","note":"Several behaviours together, not one on its own.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"17:08","label":"Hard lip compression","note":"Lips squeezed tightly together.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"23:57","label":"Answers the implied question","note":"She answers what she thinks she is being accused of, not the question asked.","cue":"Answer doesn't answer","ask":"verbal-deception"}]},{"id":"xERieqYufnU","title":"YouTuber apology videos","who":"Public apology videos by YouTubers PewDiePie, Laura Lee, Logan Paul and Tana Mongeau","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Nov 2020","dur":"56:04","sum":"Here the panel reads apology videos for sincerity rather than crimes. They point to lip puckering after ‘I’m ashamed’ (disagreeing with your own words), eyelids that close and open fast, rehearsed delivery with no hand movement, a freeze response, and tears allowed to roll on camera.","concepts":["Emotion","Stress cues","Needs & approval"],"moments":[{"t":"7:24","label":"Sour-taste lips","note":"Lip puckering right after ‘I’m ashamed’, read as disagreeing with his own statement.","cue":"Lips pulled in / bitten","ask":"cue-lips-pulled-in-bitten"},{"t":"9:29","label":"Eyelid ‘shutter’","note":"His eyes close and open fast, the fear cue Hughes calls shutter speed.","cue":"Fast eyelid 'shutter'","ask":"cue-fast-eyelid-shutter"},{"t":"10:29","label":"No hand movement","note":"A rehearsed delivery with no hand movement.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"13:19","label":"Freeze","note":"He goes still: the freeze part of fear.","cue":"Freeze","ask":"cue-freeze"},{"t":"25:11","label":"Eyes asking for approval","note":"Eye movements read as a request for approval.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"need-approval"}]},{"id":"D3YSKYAz23k","title":"Aileen Wuornos interview","who":"Aileen Wuornos, convicted serial killer (executed in 2002), in a filmed interview","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Dec 2021","dur":"1:31:29","sum":"The panel uses Wuornos’ very low blink rate as a sign of intense focus. They set her baseline for truthful, proud recall, then flag a one-shoulder shrug, lip licking right before a key phrase and chin movement (shame or grief). Their verdict on her apology is that it never actually owns the acts.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Needs & approval","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"3:48","label":"Blink rate near zero","note":"Very few blinks: intense focus.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"14:42","label":"Baseline for truthful recall","note":"Where her eyes go when she recalls something proudly and truthfully.","cue":"Looks to one side for good memories","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"15:30","label":"One-shoulder shrug","note":"She isn’t confident in what she is saying.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"1:02:08","label":"Lip compression and chin movement","note":"A first hint of shame or guilt.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"1:19:20","label":"Apology without ownership","note":"She apologises that events happened, never for what she did.","cue":"Softer word for the act ('took' not 'stole')","ask":"verbal-deception"}]},{"id":"EdCg9LAy3sY","title":"The Menendez brothers’ interviews","who":"Lyle and Erik Menendez, convicted in 1996 of killing their parents (resentenced in 2025), in interviews","src":"The Behavior Panel","by":"Chase Hughes with Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden","date":"Apr 2021","dur":"1:36:21","sum":"This episode is careful and balanced. The panel compares how long each eyebrow flash lasts, spots a flash of anger at the word ‘abuse’, and shows how rehearsed memories differ from emotional recall. They also mark stretches where his recall matches his message as good baseline.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"11:53","label":"Comparing brow flashes","note":"This flash is shorter than his others.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"cue-eyebrow-flash"},{"t":"20:21","label":"Anger at a word","note":"His brows pull down at the word ‘abuse’ in the question.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"52:52","label":"Emotional recall","note":"Hand movements and a brow flash during emotional recall.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:05:41","label":"Congruent: good baseline","note":"His recall matches the message, so it serves as good baseline.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:28:50","label":"‘We’ for bad news","note":"More team pronouns when the information is negative.","cue":"Drops 'I' from the story","ask":"pronoun-world"}]}],"others":[{"id":"KQgObU77lV8","title":"Gangsters’ body language, 1920s–50s","who":"Mobsters such as Bugsy Siegel, in arrest and courtroom photos from the 1920s to the 1950s","src":"WIRED (Tradecraft)","by":"Joe Navarro, former FBI agent","date":"Aug 2019","dur":"4:40","sum":"Navarro looks past the tough-guy image in old mobster photos. He finds a worried brow under a sharp suit, a self-hug after an arrest, a sock-pull that cools the body, a pinch at the bridge of the nose, and a lawyer’s compressed lips that seem to say the case is lost.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"1:18","label":"Self-hug after arrest","note":"Hide the face and you see a man comforting himself.","cue":"Self-hug (palms on body)","ask":"cue-self-hug-palms-on-body"},{"t":"2:19","label":"Pulling up socks","note":"Ventilating: overheated skin gets cooled.","cue":"Ventilating (collar pull, hair off neck)","ask":"cue-ventilating-collar-pull-hair-off-neck"},{"t":"2:42","label":"Eyes closed, nose pinched","note":"A near-universal ‘I don’t like this’ behaviour.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"3:09","label":"The lawyer’s compressed lips","note":"Lips pressed together with the corners turned down.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"}]},{"id":"CfNEHW3qgso","title":"Political body language","who":"US presidential candidates, mainly Trump and Biden at the 2020 debates","src":"WIRED","by":"Joe Navarro, former FBI agent","date":"Oct 2020","dur":"12:31","sum":"Navarro explains that debates are rehearsed theatre. He covers what he watches during hard questions: lip biting, collar-pulling, throat-clearing, shoulders, thumbs and steepling as signs of confidence or doubt. Then he describes Trump’s and Biden’s typical mouth and brow habits, and stresses that disappearing lips mean stress, not lying.","concepts":["Stress cues","Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"3:20","label":"What to watch on hard questions","note":"Lip biting or a collar pull as the moderator asks.","cue":"Ventilating (collar pull, hair off neck)","ask":"cue-ventilating-collar-pull-hair-off-neck"},{"t":"4:00","label":"Hesitation and shoulders","note":"Throat-clearing and a raised shoulder show low confidence.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"4:17","label":"Thumbs and steepling","note":"Thumbs up and steepled fingers show confidence. Interlaced fingers show struggle.","cue":"Steepled fingers","ask":"cue-steepled-fingers"},{"t":"9:42","label":"A speaker’s own habits","note":"A mouth-corner pinch and lip purse as personal baseline signals.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"10:49","label":"Lips disappear: stress, not lies","note":"He is explicit that this is stress, not proof of deception.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"deception-is-stress"}]},{"id":"FtC8vIpYSmM","title":"Trump’s 2016 convention speech","who":"Donald Trump (and Ivanka Trump) at the 2016 Republican National Convention","src":"Mark Bowden (TV clip)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"Jul 2016","dur":"3:13","sum":"In this short TV clip, Bowden says a teleprompter calmed Trump’s usual gestures. He reads the ‘OK’ hand as control and detail, and the chin-out profile as a ‘hit me, I can take it’ display. He also covers Ivanka’s downward intonation for authority.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"0:48","label":"Teleprompter calms gestures","note":"Reading a script reduced his usual movement.","cue":"","ask":""},{"t":"1:20","label":"The ‘OK’ hand","note":"A long-running gesture signalling control and detail.","cue":"Palms open","ask":""},{"t":"1:51","label":"Chin-out profile","note":"A ‘go on, hit me’ jaw display seen in many leaders.","cue":"Chin thrust","ask":"cue-chin-thrust"},{"t":"2:32","label":"Downward intonation","note":"A lower, falling voice gives power at the end of points.","cue":"","ask":""}]},{"id":"ymzg8LY5xGs","title":"The royal family on camera","who":"Members of the British royal family at public events (Meghan, Prince William, Prince Andrew)","src":"The Nerve with Maureen Callahan","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"Sep 2025","dur":"14:00","sum":"Bowden reads royal appearances. He points to a smile with no eye involvement, lip compression that suggests a held-back opinion, a hair-grooming display, and Prince William keeping his body turned away while Andrew whispers to him.","concepts":["Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"4:35","label":"A smile without the eyes","note":"Only the mouth corners turn up, with no narrowing of the eyes.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"},{"t":"4:52","label":"Held-back opinion","note":"Lip compression, possibly a withheld view or anger.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"6:07","label":"Grooming display","note":"Hair-touching as a display of status.","cue":"Grooming (hair, lint, straighten up)","ask":"cue-grooming-hair-lint-straighten-up"},{"t":"9:32","label":"Body stays turned away","note":"William turns his head but not his body toward Andrew, then rubs his nose.","cue":"Torso turns away","ask":"cue-torso-turns-away"}]},{"id":"yD43e5bqjV4","title":"Prince Harry’s WellChild interview","who":"Prince Harry, in an interview at the WellChild Awards","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse","date":"Oct 2026","dur":"14:29","sum":"Rouse, a Behavior Panel member, reads Harry on his own channel. He notes a grimace with tight lips and a short breath, smiles that vanish too fast to be real, a one-shoulder shrug before Harry turns to his work, and a blink flutter as a personal habit.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion","Baseline"],"moments":[{"t":"0:57","label":"Tight lips and a short breath","note":"Read as stress before an answer that needs to carry emotion.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"1:16","label":"A smile that vanishes","note":"A smile that drops too fast to be real.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"},{"t":"7:17","label":"One-shoulder shrug","note":"Read as discomfort as he moves on to his work.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"12:41","label":"Blink flutter","note":"A habit of his, so it is part of his baseline.","cue":"Fast eyelid 'shutter'","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"0g411NFWmq0","title":"Prince Harry’s GQ interview","who":"Prince Harry, in a 2025 GQ interview","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"19:22","sum":"Rouse reads Harry answer by answer. He points to a deep breath at the start, feet that look ready to leave during harder questions and relax later, a falling blink rate as stress eases, a one-shoulder shrug on an uncertain answer, and smooth, illustrated delivery on answers that look prepared.","concepts":["Stress cues","Baseline","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"0:24","label":"A big breath at the start","note":"He reads it as uncertainty about the setting, not deception.","cue":"","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"0:52","label":"Feet set to leave","note":"Foot position that looks ready to get up and go.","cue":"Feet turn to the exit","ask":"cue-feet-turn-to-the-exit"},{"t":"3:51","label":"Blink rate drops","note":"Read as stress easing as the topic changes.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"12:01","label":"One-shoulder shrug","note":"Read as uncertainty about his own answer.","cue":"Single-shoulder shrug","ask":"cue-single-shoulder-shrug"},{"t":"15:03","label":"Smooth, illustrated answer","note":"Fluent hands and narration suggest a prepared answer.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"}]},{"id":"YxKTQV5RdwU","title":"Caleb Flynn at his sentencing (NewsNation)","who":"Caleb Flynn, convicted in September 2026 of murdering his wife Ashley (Ohio), at his sentencing","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"6:46","sum":"In a NewsNation segment, Rouse discusses Flynn’s tears and trembling at sentencing. He notes squeezed eyes and tears that are never wiped, and no visible activity in the muscles usually seen in grief. He reads the distress as self-pity rather than grief, and says the display was aimed at the judge.","concepts":["Emotion","Clusters"],"moments":[{"t":"2:38","label":"Squeezed eyes, tears never wiped","note":"He reads it as pushing tears out, but notes that tears alone prove nothing.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"3:17","label":"No grief markers","note":"No movement in the chin and brow muscles usually seen in grief.","cue":"","ask":"deception-is-stress"},{"t":"3:58","label":"Trembling on cue","note":"He argues that trembling can be produced at will.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"},{"t":"4:36","label":"Upset, but about what?","note":"He says it isn't all an act; the distress is real but self-directed.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"}]},{"id":"jyN5M4i26Ig","title":"Ed Sheeran’s apology video","who":"Ed Sheeran, in a video statement about a political message at one of his concerts","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"14:39","sum":"Rouse goes through Sheeran’s statement line by line. He warns that head shakes in time with words are emphasis, not lying. He notes heavy reading of a professionally written script, a louder voice without higher pitch (so no clear stress), and illustrators that grow as he relaxes into what looks like real emotion.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"0:53","label":"Head shakes are not lies","note":"Shakes in rhythm with the words emphasise them; they don't contradict them.","cue":"","ask":"nodding"},{"t":"1:58","label":"Reading a written statement","note":"Reading a script reduces natural cues.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"8:21","label":"Louder, but not higher","note":"Volume goes up while pitch stays level, so he reads no stress.","cue":"Pitch rises slightly","ask":"cue-pitch-rises-slightly"},{"t":"11:06","label":"Illustrators grow","note":"Larger head and torso illustrators where he seems to mean it.","cue":"","ask":"composure-congruence"},{"t":"13:38","label":"Emotion once it's over","note":"Rouse reads the final emotion as genuine relief.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"Rz1qAIrjzS0","title":"Rex Heuermann’s guilty plea","who":"Rex Heuermann, who pleaded guilty to the Gilgo Beach murders (New York), at his plea hearing","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"12:32","sum":"Rouse watches Heuermann during the plea. He notes a calm he links to having given up control, and a pleasant, practised expression that fades and returns. He also points to a small head tilt and pursed lips at a point Heuermann may dispute, a slight rise in blink rate as patience wears thin, and the smile vanishing when the judge's tone hardens.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"5:15","label":"Calm after giving up control","note":"Rouse links the calm to having nothing left to decide.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"6:03","label":"A practised pleasant look","note":"One expression held throughout, which becomes his baseline.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"8:11","label":"Head tilt, pursed lips","note":"A small tilt and purse at a point he may not agree with.","cue":"Head tilt","ask":"cue-head-tilt"},{"t":"9:10","label":"Blink rate creeps up","note":"Read as patience wearing thin.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"10:04","label":"The smile disappears","note":"It fades when the judge's tone hardens.","cue":"Expression stops abruptly","ask":"cue-expression-stops-abruptly"}]},{"id":"u8NhJzynnhQ","title":"Bernard Giles’ prison interview","who":"Bernard Giles, serving life sentences for murdering five women (Florida), in a prison interview with Piers Morgan","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"22:16","sum":"Rouse highlights the near-total absence of emotion as Giles describes his crimes: nothing changes in the brow, mouth or eyes. Seat adjustments and hand-rubs appear only when questions turn to his family, and a small smile appears when he seems unsure which emotion to show. Rouse reads the stillness as control, not peace.","concepts":["Emotion","Baseline","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"0:48","label":"No emotion at all","note":"No grief, concern or sadness while admitting to the killings.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"},{"t":"6:32","label":"Brow, mouth, eyes: nothing","note":"He checks each area of the face and finds no change.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"13:46","label":"Stillness as control","note":"Calm here is control, not emotional peace.","cue":"Freeze","ask":"cue-freeze"},{"t":"20:02","label":"Hands down the legs","note":"Discomfort shows when the topic turns to his family.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"},{"t":"20:14","label":"A misplaced smile","note":"A small smile where another emotion would be expected.","cue":"","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"}]},{"id":"97arHL_QXOk","title":"Dr Gerhardt Konig at his verdict","who":"Gerhardt Konig, convicted in April 2026 of attempted manslaughter of his wife (Hawaii), as the verdict was read","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"8:44","sum":"Rouse goes through Konig’s reaction to the verdict. He notes a hand rubbed on the leg, near-freezing and a high blink rate beforehand, then zero blinks while the judge speaks. Long eye-blocking follows, read as embarrassment, along with mouth-pressing and nose-touching to self-soothe. He shows stress arriving in waves, and stresses that this is normal under such pressure.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"0:21","label":"Hand rubs the leg","note":"An adaptor that releases built-up stress.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"},{"t":"0:36","label":"Almost frozen","note":"Stillness from fear and panic before the verdict.","cue":"Freeze","ask":"cue-freeze"},{"t":"3:13","label":"Blinking stops","note":"The eyes stay wide open to take everything in.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"5:10","label":"Long eye-blocking","note":"Covering the eyes and face, read as embarrassment.","cue":"","ask":"face-touching"},{"t":"5:42","label":"Pressing the mouth","note":"Self-soothing pressure on the lips.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"}]},{"id":"BuZSpKZH7R4","title":"Justin Timberlake’s DWI stop on bodycam","who":"Justin Timberlake, on police bodycam during his 2024 arrest in New York (he later pleaded guilty to a lesser driving-while-impaired charge)","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"12:46","sum":"Rouse reads the bodycam footage of the stop. He notes raised eyebrows showing engagement, illustrators out of sync with the words, long pauses, and a low voice. Defensive hands come with lowered shoulders and hands on the thighs, which he reads as submissive, not aggressive. Later, an unblinking stare as Timberlake listens hard gives way to a slow turn away as he accepts what's happening.","concepts":["Stress cues","Baseline"],"moments":[{"t":"1:13","label":"Eyebrows up: listening","note":"Raised brows show he's engaged and respectful.","cue":"Eyebrow flash","ask":"cue-eyebrow-flash"},{"t":"3:19","label":"Illustrators out of sync","note":"Hand movements that don't land on the stressed words.","cue":"","ask":"speech-analysis"},{"t":"4:40","label":"Low voice, shoulders in","note":"Defensive hands but a calm, submissive posture.","cue":"Turtling (shoulders up, head down)","ask":"cue-turtling-shoulders-up-head-down"},{"t":"9:19","label":"Listening hard","note":"Very still, eyes wide, blink rate low.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"9:47","label":"Acceptance","note":"His head turns away and his shoulders drop.","cue":"Shoulders drop","ask":"cue-shoulders-drop"}]},{"id":"Y8t9N54fKKY","title":"Kouri Richins at her verdict","who":"Kouri Richins, convicted in March 2026 of murdering her husband Eric (Utah), as the verdict was read","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"9:09","sum":"Rouse follows Richins through the verdict. Before it, he sees adaptors such as hand-rubbing, lip and nose squishes, compressed lips and deep breaths. When the verdict is read, her eyes widen and her blink rate falls almost to zero, then her head drops and shoulders fall in defeat. He contrasts her reaction with the calmer sadness of the people around her.","concepts":["Stress cues","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"0:31","label":"Hands rubbed on the legs","note":"An adaptor for built-up tension.","cue":"Fidgeting / tapping","ask":"cue-fidgeting-tapping"},{"t":"0:51","label":"Compressed lips","note":"A strong stress sign while waiting.","cue":"Lip compression","ask":"cue-lip-compression"},{"t":"2:16","label":"Eyes widen and lock","note":"She stares at the judge and scans the jury.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"2:58","label":"Defeat posture","note":"Her head drops and her shoulders fall.","cue":"Shoulders drop","ask":"cue-shoulders-drop"},{"t":"5:01","label":"Compare the room","note":"Others' sadness looks different from hers.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"}]},{"id":"Mhk0x0oA93Q","title":"Allison Mack’s interview","who":"Allison Mack, who pleaded guilty in 2019 to racketeering charges linked to NXIVM, in an interview with Ron Rosenbaum (podcast)","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"16:36","sum":"Rouse reads Mack's interview about NXIVM. He notes disgust when she talks about the 12 years she was involved, and illustrators pulled in tight as she 'gets small'. Her blink and breathing rates rise on careful answers, her voice fades at the ends of answers, a one-sided mouth raise reads as contempt, and the grief markers are missing when she expresses regret. He reads this as self-protection, and adds an interviewing tip on silence.","concepts":["Emotion","Stress cues","Pronouns & words"],"moments":[{"t":"1:06","label":"Disgust at '12 years'","note":"A disgust expression as she names how long she was involved.","cue":"Disgust","ask":"cue-disgust"},{"t":"3:22","label":"Illustrators pull in","note":"She 'gets small' as gestures move close to her body.","cue":"Elbows pull in","ask":"cue-elbows-pull-in"},{"t":"5:59","label":"Blink and breath rate rise","note":"Stress while choosing words carefully.","cue":"Blink rate speeds up","ask":"cue-blink-rate-speeds-up"},{"t":"11:04","label":"Voice fades out","note":"Her volume drops towards the ends of answers.","cue":"","ask":"verbal-deception"},{"t":"14:23","label":"One-sided mouth raise","note":"A brief contempt display.","cue":"Contempt (one-sided smirk)","ask":"cue-contempt-one-sided-smirk"}]},{"id":"73Vd-D1gKaQ","title":"Bryan Kohberger’s traffic stop","who":"Bryan Kohberger, who pleaded guilty in 2025 to the Idaho student murders, at a traffic stop weeks after the killings (2022)","src":"Scott Rouse (YouTube)","by":"Scott Rouse, The Behavior Panel","date":"","dur":"9:19","sum":"Rouse reviews bodycam footage of a routine stop. Where most people fidget, talk a lot and blink often, Kohberger shows what Rouse calls controlled calm. His blink rate is well below normal, and his tone, posture and sentence structure all look managed. Rouse stresses that it is the whole package of small details that stands out, not any single cue.","concepts":["Baseline","Clusters","Stress cues"],"moments":[{"t":"4:14","label":"Controlled calm","note":"Unusual stillness where most people get chatty and fidgety.","cue":"Freeze","ask":"cue-freeze"},{"t":"4:36","label":"Very low blink rate","note":"Well under the normal 15–20 a minute.","cue":"Blink rate slows","ask":"cue-blink-rate-slows"},{"t":"8:35","label":"The managed package","note":"Tone, posture, word choice and blink rate together.","cue":"","ask":"clusters"}]},{"id":"8C2gVYl7Hv8","title":"Body language at the 2025 inauguration","who":"Donald Trump and Melania Trump at the January 2025 presidential inauguration (TV segment)","src":"Mark Bowden (YouTube)","by":"Mark Bowden, TruthPlane","date":"","dur":"4:28","sum":"In a short TV segment, Bowden notes that sticking to the teleprompter removed Trump's usual big gestures and faces. Melania's wide hat acted as a physical barrier to an embrace, and her eyes were hidden under it. He also points to a broad smile after the oath, which he reads as real pleasure.","concepts":["Baseline","Emotion"],"moments":[{"t":"0:22","label":"Script tames gestures","note":"On the teleprompter, his usual large gestures disappear.","cue":"","ask":"baseline"},{"t":"1:52","label":"The hat as a barrier","note":"A physical barrier that blocked a kiss and hid her eyes.","cue":"Arm or object as a barrier","ask":"cue-arm-or-object-as-a-barrier"},{"t":"3:16","label":"A real smile","note":"A broad smile after the vows, read as true pleasure.","cue":"Smile reaching the eyes","ask":"cue-smile-reaching-the-eyes"}]}],"concepts":["Baseline","Clusters","Deception cues","Stress cues","Pronouns & words","Needs & approval","Emotion"],"noteHughes":"Chase Hughes’ own analysis: mostly <i>The Behavior Panel</i>, where he analyses alongside Scott Rouse, Greg Hartley and Mark Bowden, plus a TV clip from his channel. Summaries are my own words. Timestamps were checked against each video’s captions.","noteOthers":"<b>Not Chase Hughes.</b> Other analysts, kept separate, for comparison only. Their methods and opinions are their own and not part of the Hughes-only Ask answers."};
